
  That surfaces the question everything else depends on, so it goes first.

  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mlogin[39m at [38;5;153mapp.js:4[39m is a stub with a comment saying it [3mwould[0m POST to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. It never does — it
  synchronously returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m. A server-assigned userId can only come from a real response,
  so either the stub becomes a real call or the userId stays fictional. What you pick changes the shape of every piece
  downstream:

  - [1mMake [38;5;153mlogin[39m real[0m — it becomes [38;5;153masync[39m, does [38;5;153mfetch(API_ENDPOINT)[39m, and the caller at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m becomes [38;5;153mawait[39m/[38;5;153m.then[39m. Buys
    you an actual userId and a real success/failure signal, so the tracking records something true. Costs: the submit
    handler, error handling, and any future caller all move to async — that's the expensive-to-undo part, and it's a
    real interface change. Also needs a decision about what happens when the network fails.
  - [1mKeep the stub, fake the userId[0m — the stub returns a made-up id alongside [38;5;153muser[39m. Buys you the whole tracking path
    built and testable now, with one clearly-marked seam to swap later. Costs: you're persisting invented IDs, so the
    stored data is worthless until the swap happens — fine for scaffolding, bad if anyone reads it as real.
  - [1mStub stays, tracking waits[0m — define the record shape and the storage interface, wire nothing. Cheap, reversible, but
    it ships nothing that runs.

  [1mMy recommendation: make [38;5;153mlogin[39m real.[0m The async conversion is the one genuinely irreversible piece here, and doing it
  now is far cheaper than retrofitting it under a tracking layer that already assumes synchronous returns. Faking the ID
  means building persistence against a contract you haven't confirmed.
